menu "Muse Gadget BLE audio (musegadgets protocol)"

    config MUSE_GADGET_AUTOMATION
        bool "Gadget automation tools (bench words, injected input)"
        default n
        help
            Off in release builds. MUSE_BENCH=1 (devices/sdkconfig.muse-bench)
            turns it on for lab and CI builds. On: every Nordic UART console
            word is open to any connection (!PAIR AUTO, !RESTART, !SAFEID,
            !SILENCE, !SETUP ADV, !IN ..., bench.out=, ble.pause, link.*=,
            vib=, the bench tunings). Off: a Nordic UART word that changes
            state runs only on a connection whose token proof matched, and
            answers "locked" otherwise; in SAFE, safe.off and safe=0 stay
            open. Reads (status, version, guard?, ota?, the "?" words) stay
            open either way, and so does the USB console with every word.

    config MUSE_GADGET_SEALED_CLEAR
        bool "Offer the sealed clear (mg_session_cap_sealed_clear)"
        default n
        help
            On: request_status's caps carry mg_session_cap_sealed_clear, in
            the BLE-only session and the credentials-only one, and an
            unsealed token_proof clear erases nothing (refused insecure).
            Off by default: release images offer no cap and the unsealed
            clear erases as before. The
            sealed clear itself is taken in every build. MUSE_BENCH=1
            (devices/sdkconfig.muse-bench) turns it on.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_AUDIO
        bool "musegadgets BLE audio server"
        depends on BT_NIMBLE_ENABLED && !MUSE_UI_FREEINK
        default n
        help
            Adds the musegadgets BLE protocol (protocols/mgcommands.h) to the
            BLE server Home Link already runs: the musegadgets service
            (Control and Data), Battery, Device Information and Nordic UART
            (log mirror). A phone, browser or gateway that connects and turns
            on push-to-talk gets each utterance as SBC or LC3 over BLE
            instead of it going to Muse over Wi-Fi (see the routing policy
            below). The BLE stack then stays up after setup, advertising the
            musegadgets service, and Home Link's BLE setup can skip Wi-Fi
            (token-only setup) and leaves a key for the token proof. Off by
            default; nothing changes without it.

    if MUSE_GADGET_BLE_AUDIO

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_PEER_NAME
        bool "Name the connected phone by its GAP Device Name (Status page)"
        default y
        select BT_NIMBLE_ROLE_CENTRAL
        select BT_NIMBLE_GATT_CLIENT
        help
            The Status page names the phone it's connected to: the central's GAP Device Name
            (0x2A00), as the gadget reads it, else "phone" (the read pending
            or failed; failures counted). The name is read once per
            connection, 1.5 s after connect, with NimBLE's GATT client
            (asynchronous: the host task never waits on it). That needs
            NimBLE's central role and GATT client, which this selects (the
            Muse boards otherwise build without them, devices/sdkconfig.muse);
            with the client, NimBLE confirms a phone's indications itself, so
            mg_att_confirm.c isn't linked. Off, the page says "phone"
            (client_name, setting 18, is reserved: the apps don't send it).

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_STANDALONE
        bool "BLE-only gadget: no Wi-Fi, no Muse session"
        depends on MUSE_ENABLED || HOMEHUB_VOICE
        default n
        help
            Runs the board as a musegadgets BLE gadget and nothing else:
            Wi-Fi, the Muse session over Wi-Fi, the home-network tunnel and
            over-the-air updates are never started, so their RAM stays free
            (what lets boards without PSRAM, like the Waveshare C6, run it).
            Home Link's BLE setup still runs, without Wi-Fi: until the Muse
            app has set the gadget up (its device tokens, confirmed with the
            talk button) it advertises setup beside the musegadgets service.
            Push-to-talk goes to the connected client, else to the offline
            queue, else it's refused.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_SESSION
        bool "The gadget's own VM session over BLE (device session)"
        depends on MUSE_GADGET_BLE_STANDALONE
        default y
        help
            Offers mg_command_session (protocols/mgcommands.h, Device
            session): after a matching token proof, the gadget hands the
            client its credentials, sealed, the client runs the
            gadget's Noise session to its VM, and the gadget sends and
            receives that session's messages over BLE. The gadget then does
            what a Wi-Fi gadget does on its session: registers its device
            commands (link.register), answers link.invoke, and opens its own
            requests, with the same code (main/noise_control.cpp). Turns on
            NimBLE's security manager with bonding (Just Works). Only a
            sealed hello (mgcommands.h, Sealed credentials; what the Muse apps
            send) gets the credentials, on any link; an unsealed one is
            answered insecure, bonded or not.


    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_SESSION_MAX_MESSAGE
        int "Largest VM session message the gadget takes (bytes)"
        depends on MUSE_GADGET_BLE_SESSION
        range 4096 65536
        default 16384
        help
            The device session's max_message and window: a client never sends
            a larger message, and closes the session with too_large if the VM
            does. The gadget holds this much for messages from the VM, and as
            much again while it handles one (in PSRAM when there is some),
            both only while a session is open: nothing between sessions.

    choice MUSE_GADGET_BLE_ROUTE
        prompt "Where push-to-talk goes"
        depends on !MUSE_GADGET_BLE_STANDALONE
        default MUSE_GADGET_BLE_ROUTE_AUTO
        help
            Decided once per press.

        config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_ROUTE_AUTO
            bool "BLE when a client takes it, else Wi-Fi"
            help
                A connected musegadgets client that turned on push-to-talk
                gets the utterance. Otherwise it goes to Muse over Wi-Fi as
                before. With neither, it's recorded to the offline queue if
                the client turned the queue on.

        config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_ROUTE_BLE_ONLY
            bool "BLE only"
            help
                Never Wi-Fi: the client, else the offline queue, else the
                press is refused.

        config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_ROUTE_WIFI_ONLY
            bool "Wi-Fi only"
            help
                Push-to-talk always goes to Muse over Wi-Fi. The client still
                gets gestures and can capture with start_mic.
    endchoice

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3
        bool "LC3 codec"
        default y
        help
            Offer LC3 (16 kHz, 10 ms, 40-byte frames) beside SBC: every
            gadget offers SBC and LC3. About 35 KB of
            flash. On chips without an FPU (C3, C5, C6) the fixed-point
            implementation makes it cheap: on the ESP32-C6, 0.54 ms to
            encode and 0.29 ms to decode a 10 ms frame with the codec in
            RAM (7.5 and 3.5 ms for the floating-point liblc3 on
            soft-float).

    choice MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_IMPL
        prompt "LC3 implementation"
        depends on MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3
        default MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_FIXED if !SOC_CPU_HAS_FPU
        default MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_FLOAT
        help
            Both implement the same API and the standard LC3 bitstream: each
            decodes the other's frames.

        config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_FLOAT
            bool "liblc3 (floating point)"
            help
                Google's liblc3, xplat/liblc3. About 7.5 MIPS to encode on
                the ESP32 and S3, which have a fast FPU.

        config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_FIXED
            bool "lc3fx (fixed point)"
            help
                The integer-only port of liblc3, xplat/lc3fx, for
                chips without an FPU,
                and the ESP-Mosaico's ESP32-S31 (its overlay picks it:
                integer code measured on RISC-V, bit-exact across targets).
    endchoice

    choice MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_PLACE
        prompt "LC3 code and tables"
        depends on MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3
        default MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_PLACE_FLASH
        help
            Where the LC3 codec's code and constant tables live. In flash
            they're read through the cache, which the rest of the firmware
            shares: each frame starts with misses, and on the ESP32-C6 the
            encoder runs about 2 times slower than from internal RAM.

        config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_PLACE_FLASH
            bool "Flash"

        config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_PLACE_ENC
            bool "Encoder code in IRAM (about 24 KB)"
            help
                The encoder's code and the code it shares with the decoder
                in IRAM; the decoder's own code and all tables in flash.

        config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_PLACE_TEXT
            bool "All code in IRAM (about 31 KB)"
            help
                The codec's code in IRAM, its tables in flash.

        config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_PLACE_RAM
            bool "Code in IRAM and tables in DRAM (about 57 KB)"
            help
                The whole codec in internal RAM (lc3fx: code 31 KB, the
                16 kHz tables 26 KB; linker.lf leaves the other rates'
                windows in flash): faster than code alone. The Waveshare
                C6's BLE-only profile takes it.
    endchoice

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_PLAY_BUFFER
        int "Playback buffer (bytes)"
        range 4096 65536
        default 16384
        help
            Audio the client streams to the speaker (mg_command_stream_audio)
            is held here, allocated only while a stream plays: 16 KB is 2 s
            of 60 kb/s SBC, and 500 ms at the 256 kb/s maximum bitrate.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_CAPTURE_HOLD_S
        int "Capture audio held for a slow link (seconds, with PSRAM)"
        range 1 30
        default 10
        help
            A push-to-talk capture (or a client's start_mic) is encoded into
            a hold buffer and drained as fast as the link takes it, so a turn
            on a slow link (sleep, 200 ms interval) loses nothing while the
            link moves to active. The buffer is allocated in PSRAM at the
            capture's start (this many seconds of its format: 75 KB for
            10 s of 60 kb/s SBC) and freed once everything, the end of the
            utterance included, has gone. Only when it overflows is the
            oldest audio dropped; the end of the utterance (stop_mic) is never
            dropped.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_CAPTURE_HOLD_INTERNAL_KB
        int "Capture audio held for a slow link (KB, without PSRAM)"
        range 1 64
        default 8
        help
            The hold buffer's cap where PSRAM isn't there (the C6): taken
            from internal RAM at the capture's start only while 32 KB stays
            free past it, and freed once everything has gone. 8 KB is about
            1 s of 60 kb/s SBC or 2 s of 32 kb/s LC3.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_CAPTURE_GUARD_INTERNAL_KB
        int "A bench-guard held turn's audio (KB, without PSRAM)"
        range 1 128
        default 32
        help
            The bench guard (console word ptt.minrise=N, off at every boot)
            holds a push-to-talk turn on the gadget until its capture rises
            N dB over its own floor, so it keeps all of the turn's audio
            until then. With PSRAM it takes the whole turn (16 s of its
            format); without (the C6) up to this much internal RAM, still
            only while 32 KB stays free past it. 32 KB is about 4.3 s of
            60 kb/s SBC or 8.1 s of 32 kb/s LC3; a held turn longer than
            that loses its oldest frames (counted). Off, it's never used.
            A capture held for the token proof (talk before the
            central's proof) takes the same size, in one allocation at the
            capture's start; one that fills stops the capture and keeps
            what it holds.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_VIDEO
        bool "Video streaming over BLE"
        depends on MUSE_VIDEO
        default n
        help
            Offers mg_command_stream_video: the client (the phone's camera,
            say) streams H.264 constrained baseline over BLE into its own
            ring, and muse_video (CONFIG_MUSE_VIDEO, protocols/VIDEO.md)
            decodes it and shows it full screen, as it does a Wi-Fi stream.
            The Phone camera item asks a BLE client for a stream too.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_VIDEO_BUFFER
        int "Video receive buffer (bytes, a power of two)"
        depends on MUSE_GADGET_BLE_VIDEO
        range 8192 262144
        default 65536
        help
            The client may have this much in flight; in PSRAM. 64 KB holds
            about 1 s of 500 kb/s video, enough for a keyframe and the frames
            behind it. A power of two (8192, 16384, ... 262144): the build
            fails on any other value.
            Half of it is the largest frame: a UI stream's keyframe at the
            panel's size (protocols/UI.md) takes up to ~128 KB, so UI
            streams want 262144.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_HID
        bool "HID over GATT beside musegadgets (touchscreen and pointer)"
        depends on MUSE_GADGET_BLE_AUDIO && MUSE_ENABLED
        default n
        help
            On a board with a touchscreen, the gadget is also a Bluetooth
            HID device on the same connection (HOGP, service 0x1812): a
            one-finger touchscreen (Android), an absolute pointer, or a
            relative pointer (iOS AssistiveTouch), as the client's hid_map
            picks (mg_command_ui_input, protocols/UI.md). The reports need a
            bonded, encrypted link, so this turns on NimBLE's security
            manager with bonding (Just Works) and keeps bonds in NVS; the
            phone pairs from its Bluetooth settings or when an app reads the
            Report Map. Advertising adds the HID UUID and appearance.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LIVE_CALL
        bool "Live call with the Muse"
        depends on MUSE_ENABLED
        default y if SPIRAM
        help
            Offers mg_command_live_call: Live call in Settings (or the menu)
            asks the connected client to start a live voice call with the
            Muse, and the screen shows the call while the client says it's
            connecting or live, with a button to end it. Push-to-talk and
            replies work as outside a call, and video the client streams
            during the call shows without opening Phone camera.
            On by default only where a call works: the full-UI BLE boards
            with PSRAM (the call's picture, controls and subtitles live
            there). Elsewhere it's compiled out: no Call disc on the home,
            no menu row.

    config MUSE_CALL_AT_BOOT
        string "Bench: a timed call armed at boot (DELAY_S,LEN_S)"
        depends on MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LIVE_CALL
        default ""
        help
            Bench images only. Empty: off. "DELAY_S,LEN_S"
            arms the console's call.at at boot, once per image: a phone app
            that holds the gadget's only link and never writes its console
            still gets a timed call (DELAY_S after its session is ready,
            LEN_S long, ended by the gadget). It fires once for each image
            (its ELF sha256, kept in NVS), never on that image's later boots.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3_24K
        bool "List LC3 at 24 kHz"
        depends on MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LC3
        default n
        help
            LC3 playback at 24 kHz in stream_audio's
            rates, and 24 kHz captures where the board's audio bus takes
            them. Off until the board's check (encode and decode time, the
            mic filter on real audio) has passed: the Muse apps use 24 kHz
            whenever it's listed. The code is built and host-tested either way.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_SBC_BITPOOL
        int "SBC bitpool"
        range 2 60
        default 26
        help
            16 kHz mono, 16 blocks, 8 subbands. 26 gives 60-byte frames
            every 8 ms (60 kb/s), the quality of mSBC.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_QUEUE
        bool "Offline clip queue"
        default y
        help
            Record push-to-talk to flash while no client is connected, when
            the client turned audio_queue_enabled on, and hand the clips over
            later. Needs the mg_queue partition (partitions_muse.csv has
            one); without it the queue isn't offered.

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_LINK_WAKE
        bool "Listen at every connection event during a link-mode switch, by default (experimental)"
        default n
        help
            Experimental. While a link-mode switch is
            pending, the gadget can ignore its slave latency locally so the
            central's answer is heard at the next event (the S3 and C3
            controllers' vendor command 0xFC13; elsewhere a pending
            notification). It's on by default only where an A/B shows
            a gain. Measured (talk press from a sleep link to the active
            grant, link.wake 1 vs 0): ESP32-S31 + iPhone 17 2976 vs 2974 ms
            (n=3+3); ESP32-S3 + Galaxy S22 10.7 vs 11.1 s (n=3+3, the phone's
            spread 7-14 s). No gain on either, so it's off by default. The
            bench word link.wake=1 turns it on for the boot (RAM only).

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_SMP
        bool "Firmware updates over BLE (MCUmgr SMP)"
        default y if MUSE_GADGET_BLE_AUDIO && !MUSE_BOARD_ESP_MOSAICO
        default n
        help
            The MCUmgr SMP service the Muse apps'
            firmware update speaks (OS echo/reset/params, image state and
            upload), the SMP core (smp/, vendored) under the
            ESP-IDF backend (mg_smp_esp.c). The file is an MCUboot envelope
            around the signed ESP-IDF app (tools/muse/mcuboot_wrap.py makes
            one); it goes into the other OTA slot, ESP-IDF checks it and its
            signature, the client marks it to test and resets, and the image
            is kept at the boot's healthy mark (with or without the client's
            confirm); a test image that never gets there boots the old one
            again. Answers in every mode, SAFE included: it is the BLE
            recovery tier. Needs two OTA slots (partitions_muse*.csv): off
            on the ESP-Mosaico, which updates through ESP-Iris over USB.
            Reads (echo, image state, params) are open. An upload, image
            state write, reset or erase needs no phone or token: a
            connection whose token proof matched, the wired console's
            update.arm, a SAFE the user entered with the key, and bench,
            automation and test builds take it at once; any other link,
            set up or not, gets one confirm press on the gadget ("Press to
            confirm update", rc 10 while it asks, rc 11 if it times out or
            is cancelled). A SAFE the gadget entered itself can't prompt
            and refuses (rc 11). The image's signature
            check protects it too (the dev key is in the repo). Before the
            first byte is written, the upload's app header must name this
            chip, product code and board profile (components/muse_image_id),
            or the upload is refused.

    config MUSE_GADGET_WIFI_OTA_SAME_BOARD
        bool "Accept a Wi-Fi OTA (Link setup's) of this board's own image"
        depends on HOMEHUB_OTA_ENABLED && !MUSE_GADGET_BLE_STANDALONE
        default n
        help
            A Muse Gadget updates over Bluetooth (MCUmgr SMP), from the
            Muse apps or a web updater. Off (the default), it refuses every
            Wi-Fi OTA: Link setup's ota_url and its device.ota action are
            ignored; each refusal is logged with the URL's host and path
            (never its query). An image built for an ESP32-S3 with the dev
            key would pass both the chip id and the signature checks. On, an
            image is installed only when its board description (components/
            muse_image_id) names this exact board, the same product code and
            board profile, read from the download's first bytes before
            anything is written; ESP-IDF's chip id and signature checks
            stay. Either way a re-setup of a set-up gadget never takes an
            ota_url (ble_server.c ignores it).

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_MANUFACTURER
        string "Manufacturer name (Device Information)"
        default "Waveshare" if MUSE_BOARD_WAVESHARE_S3_175C || MUSE_BOARD_WAVESHARE_S3_175 || MUSE_BOARD_WAVESHARE_S3_216 || MUSE_BOARD_WAVESHARE_S3_185C || MUSE_BOARD_WAVESHARE_C6_18 || MUSE_BOARD_WAVESHARE_C6_206 || MUSE_BOARD_WAVESHARE_S3_LCD7 || MUSE_BOARD_WAVESHARE_S3_LCD128
        default "Seeed Studio" if MUSE_BOARD_SENSECAP_WATCHER || MUSE_BOARD_RETERMINAL_STICKY
        default "M5Stack" if MUSE_BOARD_M5STACK_STOPWATCH || MUSE_BOARD_M5STACK_STICKS3 || MUSE_BOARD_M5STACK_STICKC_PLUS2 || MUSE_BOARD_M5STACK_CORES3 || MUSE_BOARD_M5STACK_CORE2 || MUSE_BOARD_M5STACK_CARDPUTER_ADV
        default "Freenove" if MUSE_BOARD_FNK0104B
        default "Guition" if MUSE_BOARD_GUITION_JC3248W535
        default "FoloToy" if MUSE_BOARD_AI_PASSPORT
        default "OSTB" if MUSE_BOARD_OSTB_ECHOEAR_2ST
        default "Nabu Casa" if HOMEHUB_VOICE
        default "Espressif"
        help
            Who makes the hardware, never the chip vendor:
            M5Stack for the StopWatch, Seeed Studio for the SenseCAP
            Watcher, Waveshare for its boards. Espressif only for boards
            Espressif makes (ESP-Mosaico, ESP32-S3-BOX-3, the DevKits). The
            other Device Information strings: Model = the board's name
            (muse_board_t.name), Firmware Revision = the image's version,
            Software Revision = mg<MG_SPEC_VERSION> (mg1).

    config MUSE_GADGET_BLE_NUS_LOG_TAGS
        string "Log tags mirrored on the Nordic UART service"
        default "mg,link.main,link.heartbeat,muse_audio"
        help
            Comma-separated tag prefixes, or * for the whole log. muse_audio
            says when the codecs can't open (no DMA memory, a held lock), which
            a board without its USB cable shows nowhere else. The
            Nordic UART service has no security, so anyone nearby can read
            it: keep tags that can log what people say or type (voice
            transcripts, chat) out of it.

    endif

endmenu
